breaking_changes.Adaptation_method:
Ensure all option flags follow the supported format:
- Single character flags should use a single dash (e.g., '-a')
- Multi-character flags should use double dashes (e.g., '--abc')
- Define each long option separately instead of combining multiple long options in a single Option

Example - change this:
```javascript
// Invalid format that now throws an error
new Option('-abc', 'description');
// Or multiple long options
new Option('--es --ecmascript', 'description');
```

To this:
```javascript
// Valid format
new Option('-a', 'description');
new Option('--abc', 'description');
// For multiple long options, define them separately
new Option('--es', 'description');
new Option('--ecmascript', 'description');
```

Note: Support for dual long options was re-added in Commander 13.1 after this breaking change in 13.0.

If you need to make multiple calls to `.parse()` when using `storeOptionsAsProperties: true`, you can use the newly added `.saveStateBeforeParse()` and `.restoreStateBeforeParse()` methods to maintain state between parse calls. First save the state, then parse, and restore the state if needed for another parse:
```javascript
// When storeOptionsAsProperties: true
command.saveStateBeforeParse();
command.parse(args1);
// Do something with the parsed results
command.restoreStateBeforeParse();
command.parse(args2);
```
Alternatively, you can avoid multiple parse calls or set `storeOptionsAsProperties: false` which now automatically handles multiple parse calls.
